# Getting started

Odroe is currently developed from source. Use a path dependency while the
public package is being prepared.

## Create the product

Start with a normal Flutter project so the same product can reach Android,
iOS, and Web.

```sh
flutter create --platforms=android,ios,web my_app
cd my_app
```

Add the source checkout:

```yaml
dependencies:
  odroe:
    path: ../odroe
```

Run dependency resolution with Flutter because the single package includes
Flutter product entrypoints.

```sh
flutter pub get
```

## Define the shared route

Every route keeps its shared contract in `route.dart`. A Document is attached
there as a platform-neutral binding, so the server can return useful HTML
without importing Flutter.

```dart
import 'package:odroe/document.dart';
import 'package:odroe/router.dart';

final route =
    AppRoute<NoParams, NoSearch, NoData>().document(
      (_) => const RouteDocument(
        title: 'Hello',
        body: HtmlElement(
          'h1',
          children: <HtmlNode>[HtmlText('Hello from Odroe')],
        ),
      ),
    );
```

Save this as `lib/routes/route.dart`. The directory becomes the URL:
`lib/routes/about/route.dart` maps to `/about`.

## Add the Flutter page

Save the runtime-specific UI as `lib/routes/page.dart`:

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:odroe/router_flutter.dart';

import 'route.dart' as definition;

final route = definition.route.page(
  build: (_) => const Scaffold(
    body: Center(child: Text('Hello from Flutter')),
  ),
);
```

Compose the generated route tree in `lib/main.dart`:

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:odroe/document_flutter.dart';
import 'package:odroe/odroe_flutter.dart';
import 'package:odroe/router_flutter.dart';

import 'routes.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(
    App(
      modules: <Module>[
        DocumentModule(),
        RouterModule(routes: routeTree),
      ],
      builder: (app) => MaterialApp.router(
        routerConfig: app.read(routerKey),
      ),
    ),
  );
}
```

## Generate and run

```sh
dart run odroe generate
dart run odroe dev --server-only
```

The server-only command renders the Document from `route.dart`. Development
serves files directly from `public/` and never reuses stale `build/web` HTML.

For Flutter, pass Flutter arguments after `--`:

```sh
flutter devices
dart run odroe dev -- -d chrome
dart run odroe dev -- -d <ios-device-id>
```

Replace `<ios-device-id>` with the device identifier reported by Flutter.

## Build

```sh
dart run odroe build web
dart run odroe build apk
dart run odroe build --server-only
```

`--server-only` emits only the native server. A product with no Flutter
`page.dart` can run `dart run odroe build` without a target; Odroe emits the
native server and prerenders every known static location into `build/web`.
Known locations come from static file routes and `lib/prerender.dart`; link
crawling is disabled by default. Add `--prerender-crawl` only when page links
should discover extra same-origin HTML. See [Deployment](/docs/deploy) for
budgets, static-root behavior, and platform targets.
